  "hostname": "BL-LT-0112",
  "reason": "Confirm laptop is online/connected for sign-in-screen password change"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, thanks for reaching out. I checked your account. It's active and not locked, and it's set up to ask you for a new password the next time you sign in, which is expected. Your three tries haven't locked you out. When the new password gets an error, it's usually because the password doesn't meet the rules. Please try this: 1) Change it on your laptop's Windows sign-in screen, not on a web page, and make sure you're connected to the office network or VPN first. 2) Make your new password at least 14 characters long, and don't reuse a password you've had before. A short phrase works well, for example three or four words with a number. 3) Check that Caps Lock is off and your keyboard language is right. Watch for characters that look alike, like l and I, or 0 and O, when you type the temporary password. If you still get an error, please reply and tell me where you were signing in (Windows screen or a web page) and the exact error message. A screenshot would be great. If the temporary password itself turns out not to work, we can have a new one issued and given to you by phone.",
  "internal_note": "KB-003 quick checks: user_id 12. Account is active, must_change_password=true, failed_login_count=0, not blocked, VPN access enabled. Temp password was set 2026-10-11 02:57 UTC. Sign-in logs show no failed password attempts and all sign-ins came from NY, corporate and residential networks. Nothing suspicious. Laptop BL-LT-0112 is online with no warnings. Most likely cause is the new password failing policy (at least 14 chars, no reuse) or the change being tried on a web page or off the network. I sent the user the KB-003 fix steps and asked where she's signing in and for the exact error. No unlock needed. If the temp password truly fails, issue a new one per KB-002. Escalate if the account turns out active with no change required and no password works. Note: the logs show successful sign-ins going back to 9/28 with MFA 'none', which is a bit odd for a new hire. You may want to check her Duo enrollment as part of onboarding.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-003"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
